{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2020",
    "useDefineForClassFields": true,
    "module": "ESNext",
    /*
      No DOM: every web global this package names — `fetch`, `Response`, `AbortSignal`, `URL` — is read from
      `@types/node` (undici-types), which is also the flavour `tencent-doc-sdk` compiles against. Adding DOM
      would bind a second, browser-shaped declaration over each of those names, and would additionally need
      `DOM.Iterable` before `FetcherHeaders`' iterator members exist at all.
      This is no longer about the transport seam. The seam is `WebFetcher` from `universal-fetch-type`, whose
      request body is DOM-compatible, so a DOM program can hand in its own `fetch` unwrapped — that direction
      is judged by `tests/xiv-datamine-polyfill-e2e-test/test/fetcher-variance.ts`, not by this file.
    */
    "lib": ["ES2020"],
    "types": ["node"],
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    /* `@/…` is a module under `src/`, `@test/…` one under `test/`; the aliases are declared for vite in
       vite.config.ts, which is what vitest and the bundle resolve through, and `tsconfig.build.json` narrows
       the same mapping to `src/` alone because that is the public surface. Declarations are generated in the
       bundling pass by `unplugin-dts`, which resolves the aliases itself — a plain tsc emit would copy
       `@/…` verbatim into a file no consumer can read. */
    "paths": {
      "@/*": ["./src/*"],
      "@test/*": ["./test/*"]
    },
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "noEmit": true,
    "resolveJsonModule": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "noUncheckedSideEffectImports": true
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.ts", "src/**/*.json", "test/**/*.ts", "vite.config.ts"]
}
